This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon. Split it into two separate sentences.

Rewrite for example:

```ts
// Scoped selector: subscribing to the whole derived value re-renders this view on any report-action
// change anywhere in the app. The selector keeps the ref stable while this report's slice is unchanged.
```

This added JSDoc comment uses an em dash. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without stylistic em dashes.

Rewrite it without the dash, e.g.:

```ts
/** The Button's press handler. `ButtonKeyboardShortcut` fires it when Enter is pressed. */
onPress: (event?: GestureResponderEvent | KeyboardEvent) => void | Promise<void>;
```

This newly added JSDoc comment uses an em dash to join two clauses. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without stylistic em dashes.

Split it into two plain sentences, e.g.:

```ts
/** Whether the Button is loading, either external or from the immediate-press mechanism. `ButtonKeyboardShortcut` uses it to block the Enter shortcut. */
isLoading: boolean;
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...ordinary unread indicators; ephemeral draft events cannot be recovered from history.`). Comments should read as plain, natural sentences and use two separate sentences instead of a semicolon.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// This state belongs to the requesting browser session, not to a mounted chat. Reopening the app
// falls back to ordinary unread indicators. Ephemeral draft events cannot be recovered from history.
```

This newly added comment uses a semicolon to join two independent sentences ("...it carries no pending state of its own; only a merchant rule can already be on its way out."). Comments should read as plain sentences and split independent clauses into separate sentences rather than stitching them with a semicolon.

Rewrite as two sentences:

```ts
// A category tax rule is its category, so it carries no pending state of its own. Only a merchant rule can
// already be on its way out. Declared above the not-found returns below, since a hook can't run conditionally.
```

This comment joins two independent sentences with a semicolon. Use a period between the two complete sentences instead.

```ts
// A short key could match unrelated numbers as substrings. 10 digits exceeds real accountIDs and the small
// integers request data usually holds. A genuine optimistic ID this short (~1 in 9 million) is left to the server.
```

This comment joins two independent sentences with a semicolon. Comments should read as plain, natural prose and use a period between two complete sentences instead of a semicolon.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// deepReplaceKeysAndValues only rewrites strings. This extra pass covers the accountID sent as a number
// (e.g. the agentAccountID parameter).
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`), which the plain-comment-style rule disallows.

Replace the em dash with plain sentence punctuation, e.g.:
```ts
// height, which is what it always had. Do not "fix" it to `lineHeightScale.micro` (14/16), because that
// changes the rendered line height.
```

This newly added comment uses em dashes (`—`), which the plain-comment-style rule disallows. Comments should read as plain sentences without stylistic tics like em dashes.

Rewrite the two clauses as separate sentences, e.g.:
```ts
// `h1` is the 22/28 size. The legacy `fontSizeH1` variable was 19, the same value as `fontSizeH2`, so
// anything that used to read `fontSizeH1` belongs on `h2`, not here. Do not reintroduce a separate 19px "H1".
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This one joins two independent statements with a semicolon (`...before layout; a passive effect has no such guarantee.`), which the style rule calls out. Split it into two sentences.

```ts
// Call from a layout effect. React runs those inside the commit, before layout. A passive effect has no such guarantee.
function markSendMessageCommitted(reportActionID: string | undefined) {
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`The result is ignored; the link inside the prompt closes the modal and opens a chat with the workspace owner.`). Comments should read as plain, natural sentences, and a semicolon should be split into two separate sentences.

Rewrite as two sentences, e.g.:

```tsx
// Acknowledgement-only modal shown when the current user isn't allowed to share the bank account. The result is ignored.
// The link inside the prompt closes the modal and opens a chat with the workspace owner.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`The result is ignored; the only actionable path is the link inside the prompt...`). Comments should read as plain, natural sentences, and a semicolon should be split into two separate sentences.

Rewrite as two sentences, e.g.:

```tsx
// Acknowledgement-only modal shown when the bank account still needs to be validated. The result is ignored.
// The only actionable path is the link inside the prompt, which closes the modal before navigating away.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...the time-sensitive task); the status itself stays truthful...`). Per our comment style, a semicolon should be split into two separate sentences so the comment reads as plain, natural prose.

Split it into two sentences instead:

```tsx
// The grace period only stops us from prompting the user (the RBR and the time-sensitive task). The status
// itself stays truthful, so a long-broken card still reads as Inactive with a message it can be fixed from.
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...restricted from static import; this hook never touches them...`), which the plain-comment-style rule flags — a semicolon should be split into two separate sentences.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// isPaidGroupPolicy/isPaidGroupPolicyExpenseReport are billing-only and restricted from static import.
// This hook never touches them, but a namespace import can't be statically proven not to, so these
// ReportUtils spies go through require() instead (matches other tests spying on this module).
```

The comment uses an em dash (`...this test never renders them — only the back-navigation targets...`). Write the relationship as a plain sentence instead of an em dash.

```ts
// The remaining steps pull in native modules (Plaid, Onfido) that cannot load under Jest, and this test never renders
// them. Only the back-navigation targets they contribute to the step order matter.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash ("under report \"0\" — those used to fall through"). Write it as plain sentences without the em dash.

Example fix:
```ts
// No thread yet. The shared resolver creates one, including for an unreported (self-DM) expense, whose IOU
// action lives in the self-DM rather than under report "0". Those used to fall through with no target at
// all and dump the user in their self-DM.
```

This comment uses a semicolon to join two statements (`pathological on prebuilt minified bundles; worklet-containing libs still get the plugin`). Use two sentences.

Example:

```js
// React Compiler. Skips hermes-parser, which is pathological on prebuilt minified bundles.
// Worklet-containing libs still get the plugin via worklets-loader's sniff.
```

This comment uses arrows (`fullstory (needs JSX) → oxc → worklets → CJS lowering`) instead of writing the relationship in words. Describe the order in plain prose.

Example:

```js
// runs through the same Rust compiler on both platforms. Loaders run bottom-up: fullstory (which
// needs JSX), then oxc, then worklets, then CJS lowering with inlined requires.
```

This comment uses a semicolon to join two statements (`so the node_modules rule opts into lowering those too; everything else modern Hermes handles natively`). Use two sentences.

Example:

```js
// node_modules also ship async generators, which Hermes cannot parse, so the node_modules rule
// opts into lowering those too. Everything else modern Hermes handles natively.
```

This comment uses an em dash (`which remapping() below rejects — the node_modules rule passes \`sourcemap: false\``). Split into two sentences.

Example:

```js
// On prebuilt bundles SWC can emit a multi-source map, which remapping() below rejects. The
// node_modules rule passes `sourcemap: false` to skip maps entirely.
```

This comment uses a dash (`stop the module loading at all - side-effect imports rely on it`) and a semicolon (`Metro's plugin does remove these; staying conservative here`). Split into plain sentences.

Example:

```js
// With no references there is nothing to inline into, and dropping the declaration would stop the
// module from loading at all, since side-effect imports rely on it. Metro's plugin does remove
// these, but we stay conservative here.
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...the rate is no longer on the policy; it never checks \`enabled\`.`), which the plain-comment-style rule flags — use two separate sentences instead.

Split the clause into two sentences:

```ts
// The backend flags CUSTOM_UNIT_OUT_OF_POLICY for distance only when the rate is no longer on the
// policy. It never checks `enabled`. A disabled-but-present distance rate is still valid. Only a rate
// pending deletion is not (deletePolicyDistanceRates disables it and marks it DELETE optimistically).
// Per diem keeps flagging a disabled rate as before.
```

This newly added prop comment uses an em dash to join two clauses. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Rewrite without the em dash, for example:
```ts
/** Whether the user has completed the guided setup flow. Used by the offline-recovery `openReport` call. */
hasCompletedGuidedSetupFlow: boolean | undefined;
```

This newly added prop comment uses an em dash to join two clauses. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Rewrite without the em dash, for example:
```ts
/** Whether the user has seen the self tour. Used by the offline-recovery `openReport` call. */
isSelfTourViewed: boolean | undefined;
```

This added comment uses em dashes (`—`) to set off a parenthetical clause. The rule asks for plain sentences without em/en dashes.

Rewrite the aside as its own sentence or wrap it in commas:

```ts
// configureAndSubscribe so ApiUtils' ACTIVE_SERVER callback, the source of truth for getApiRoot(),
// has updated its cached value first. Sampling it early bakes the wrong reachabilityUrl into NetInfo.
```

This added comment uses an em dash (`—`), which the plain-comment-style rule flags. Comments should read as plain sentences without em/en dashes.

Replace the em dash with a period or comma so the thought is two clauses or two sentences:

```ts
// carry the bearer and would read as a permanent outage. NetInfo's `reachabilityHeaders` is not a way
// out. That config is static and the access token rotates on Cloudflare's schedule.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences; use two separate sentences instead of a semicolon.

```ts
// Kept separate from `targetOwnerAccountID`, which stops at the first owner it finds. Counting needs them all.
const hasMultipleSubmitters = useMemo(() => {
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) in its own sentence. Rewrite it without the em dash:

```ts
// Mirror image of the complete case. `reopenTask` writes only to `report_<taskID>`, so the snapshot is
// left claiming the task is still completed.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) in its own sentence. Rewrite it as two plain sentences:

```ts
// The snapshot still says the task is open. This is exactly what `completeTask` leaves behind,
// because it only writes to `report_<taskID>` and never patches `snapshot_<hash>`.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) in its own sentence. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without em dashes.

Split it into two sentences instead:

```ts
// Drop tasks whose live status no longer matches the active `status:` filter. The snapshot still lists a
// just-completed task under `status:outstanding` because `completeTask` never writes to it.
.filter((task) => isEligibleForTaskStatus(currentQueryJSON, task));
```

The added comment uses an em dash: `...it can only do so when CONCIERGE_REPORT_ID is already cached — on the create/open path it is not...`. Write it as two plain sentences instead.

```ts
// button. Mirrors the {reportID} context the side panel builds on web. The button drops a self-referencing
// source report, but it can only do so when CONCIERGE_REPORT_ID is already cached. On the create/open path
// it is not, so guard here as well (reportID is the Concierge report by the check above).
```

The added comment uses an em dash: `...scoped to Concierge opened via this sidebar button — search/LHN/deep links never carry it.`. Comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Split it into two sentences:

```tsx
// Capture the report the user is viewing (still topmost at press time) so Concierge can act on it
// after we navigate away. This is the only entry that threads a source report, so context is scoped
// to Concierge opened via this sidebar button. Search, LHN, and deep links never carry it.
```

This newly added comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...just the username; otherwise show the full email`). Comments should read as plain, natural prose, so a semicolon splicing two thoughts together should be two separate sentences instead.

Split it into two sentences:

```tsx
// When the emails share a private domain we can strip the domain and show just the username. Otherwise show the full email.
const userHandle = areEmailsFromSamePrivateDomain(login, currentUserPersonalDetails.login ?? '') ? login.split('@').at(0) : formatPhoneNumber(login);
```

This comment uses a semicolon to join two independent statements (`Native echoes one stale event after a controlled selection update; consume it once.`). Split it into two separate sentences to keep the comment reading as plain prose.

```ts
// Native echoes one stale event after a controlled selection update. Consume it once. Only ever armed on the
// platforms that echo, so no platform check is needed where it is read.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) to join two clauses. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without em dashes.

Split it into separate sentences, for example:

```tsx
// Block deleting a value only for a field imported from the accounting connection, not for every field on a
// connected workspace. Manual field values stay deletable while connected, and there is no field during creation.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) to join two clauses. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without em dashes.

Split it into separate sentences, for example:

```tsx
// Block value management only for a field imported from the accounting connection, not for every field on a
// connected workspace. Manual fields stay editable while connected, and there is no field yet during creation.
```

This newly added comment uses em dashes to set off a parenthetical clause (`render in that same wide RHP — the invoice preview card already opens them there — so they must pass this guard too`). Comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Suggested fix (rewrite without dashes):

```ts
// ParentNavigationSubtitle. Invoice reports render in that same wide RHP, and the invoice preview card already opens
// them there, so they must pass this guard too, which isMoneyRequestReport (IOU || Expense) alone does not do.
```

This newly added comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`MoneyRequestReportActionsList; a report holding a single expense renders this list instead`). Comments should read as plain sentences, and a semicolon should be split into two separate sentences.

Suggested fix:

```ts
// align-to-top mount that money-request and invoice reports normally get. Multi-expense reports get this from
// MoneyRequestReportActionsList. A report holding a single expense renders this list instead, so it has to honor
// the same route param. The value is latched on mount because clearing the param below must not flip the list
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon instead of writing them as two separate sentences.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// Empty is a real value for submit and reimbursement-amount tokens. Keep it instead of falling back to the raw definition.
if (value === '' && !isSubmissionInfoPart(part) && !isReimbursementAmountPart(part)) {
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon instead of writing them as two separate sentences.

Split the semicolon into two sentences, for example:

```ts
// An expense report links its workspace chat via `chatReportID`. `parentReportID` covers shapes that only carry the parent link, which normally points at the same chat.
const chatReportID = getNonEmptyStringOnyxID(report?.chatReportID) ?? getNonEmptyStringOnyxID(report?.parentReportID);
```

This newly added comment uses a semicolon to join two independent clauses. The plain-comment style guideline prefers two separate sentences instead.

Split it into two sentences, for example:
```ts
// default workflow at all. Fall back to the chain starting at the default approver. The next save of that
// workflow writes the marker and settles it for good.
```

This comment (relocated into the diff) uses an em dash to join two clauses, which the plain-comment-style rule flags. Comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Split it into two sentences, e.g.:

```ts
// `common.error.invalidAmount` is only surfaced inline when the editable amount input is rendered. Distance requests
// disable that input (the amount falls back to a read-only menu row that doesn't show this error), so keep the
// distance-amount validation error in the footer. Otherwise an invalid distance expense would fail silently.
```

This comment uses an em dash, which the plain-comment-style rule flags.

Replace the em dash with a plain sentence break, e.g.:
```ts
// The Submit workspace is auto-created for this intent, so unlike the other intents there is no "Create a workspace"
// step to show. It would land pre-checked and add nothing.
```

This comment uses an em dash, which the plain-comment-style rule flags. Comments should read as plain sentences without em dashes.

Rewrite the em dash as separate clauses or sentences, e.g.:
```ts
// Submit workspaces use a flat role model. `getRoleForCallerOnNewPolicy` hands even the creator the `editor` role, and
// `updateWorkspaceMembersRole` refuses to change it, so `isPolicyAdmin` is never true for them. Gate the Submit intent on
// ownership instead: true for the workspace auto-created during onboarding, false for members invited to someone else's.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) in its own sentence (`...one pass it — the rest get the first sentence alone...`). Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without stylistic tics like em dashes.

Split it into two sentences instead:

```ts
// fallbackVendorName is the vendor the integration auto-creates when nothing matches. Only integrations
// that actually create one pass it. The rest get the first sentence alone, because telling a Xero admin
// their expenses will export as "Credit Card Misc." describes a fallback Xero doesn't have.
```

This comment joins two independent sentences with a semicolon, which the plain-comment-style guideline flags. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences, using a period between independent clauses instead of a semicolon.

Split the clause into two sentences:

```ts
// These wide-RHP reports open scrolled to the top. Opening from the "X Replies" link should instead land on the
// latest message, so we append the shouldScrollToLatest flag for the money-request branches.
```

The comment uses an em dash (`—`) in its own sentence, which the plain-comment-style guideline avoids. Comments should read like plain, natural sentences without stylistic tics such as em dashes.

Replace the em dash with two separate sentences or a plain connector, e.g.:

```ts
// User clicks into a composer and types. Pointerdown puts us in mouse modality, so focusin never
// records lastInteractiveElement, and Cmd+Shift+K latches no Enter/Space activation.
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without an em dash. This comment uses an em dash (`missing category — the edit icon is hidden`) to join two clauses.

Replace the em dash with two separate sentences:
```ts
// the list. Without this the cell deadlocks on an expense with a missing category. The edit icon is
// hidden, so the picker never mounts and the categories are never fetched.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon instead of writing them as two separate sentences, which is one of the stylistic tics CONSISTENCY-16 asks us to avoid.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// Only the thread-creation boundary is replaced. Everything else in the Report actions module stays real.
jest.mock('@userActions/Report', () => ({
    ...jest.requireActual<typeof ReportActions>('@userActions/Report'),
    createTransactionThreadReport: jest.fn(),
}));
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon, which the style guide asks you to write as two separate sentences instead.

Split the clauses into two sentences:

```ts
// A later write may have opened a new gate since. Resolving that one would let READs through
// while somebody else's write is still pending.
if (isReadyPromise !== claimedPromise) {
    return;
}
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, use two separate sentences instead of a semicolon so the comment reads like natural prose.

Suggested fix:

```tsx
// The server has already moved the payer. Refetch so the local policy stops reporting the former owner as payer.
useEffect(() => {
    openPolicyProfilePage(policyID);
}, [policyID]);
```

Comments should read as plain, natural sentences. This comment uses a semicolon to splice two independent clauses (`... the word text alone can't); the array position is what makes it unique.`) instead of writing them as two separate sentences.

Split the clause into two sentences:

```tsx
// The word list is derived synchronously from a fixed translation and is never reordered, inserted into, or
// filtered, so a word's index is a stable identity. wordIndex is only needed to disambiguate repeated words
// within a run, since the word text alone can't. The array position is what makes it unique.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...system message thread; opening the paid expense...`). Comments should read like plain, natural sentences, using two separate sentences instead of a semicolon.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// The pay action's childReportID is the "marked as paid" system message thread. Opening the paid
// expense must land on the report itself, not that thread.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...it can't be derived locally); read it from the snapshot here.`) instead of writing them as two separate sentences. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, comments should read as natural sentences without semicolon-joined clauses.

Split it into two sentences, for example:

```tsx
// For expense-report searches `count` is the number of expenses, not reports, so it can't label the Reports tab.
// The matching-report total across all pages is server-provided. `selectedTransactions` only holds the loaded
// page, so it can't be derived locally. Read it from the snapshot here.
```

This comment uses `->` arrows to describe the import cycle instead of writing the relationship out in words. Per the plain-comment-style rule, relationships should be spelled out as ordinary prose rather than expressed with arrows.

Rewrite the chain in words, for example:

```ts
// user action imports libs/API. Registering from inside libs/API therefore closes an import cycle:
// libs/API imports Middleware, Middleware imports an action, and that action imports libs/API again.
// Instead the composition root imports this module for its side effect, before anything can call
// processWithMiddleware. See src/setup/index.ts.
```

This newly added prop comment uses an em dash, which the plain-comment-style rule flags. Rewrite it as a plain sentence without the em dash.

```tsx
/** Active policy read by sections. It may differ from the context `policyID` in track-expense flows where the user moves the expense to a different workspace. */
policy: OnyxEntry<OnyxTypes.Policy>;
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (`—`) mid-sentence. Comments should be plain sentences without em/en dashes.

Rephrase without the dash, e.g.:

```ts
// The reimbursable totals are what the report preview and details actually read, so they are
// overridable in their own right. `total` alone leaves them pinned at whatever they were seeded with.
```

This newly added comment uses em dashes (`—`) to set off a clause. Comments should be plain sentences without em/en dashes.

Rephrase without the dashes, e.g.:

```ts
// A `new*Total` override is already expressed in the report's currency, so unlike the raw per-transaction
// arithmetic below, it does not need the transaction's own currency to match the report's. That guard is
// precisely why an expense in another currency otherwise never reaches the total.
```

This comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, use two separate sentences instead of a semicolon.

Rewrite as separate sentences, e.g.:

```ts
// ...Not narrower, since a truncated amount reads as a different amount rather than as a truncation. Not wider either,
// since the spare room belongs to the free-text columns.
```

This newly added comment uses an em dash (—) between "A superset of RESERVATION_TYPE" and "HOTEL_BLOCK has no counterpart...". Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without em/en dashes.

Replace the em dash with two separate sentences:

```ts
// The travel-booking classifier's output. A superset of RESERVATION_TYPE. HOTEL_BLOCK has no counterpart in
// an actual Spotnana reservation, since a hotel block is by definition never booked through Expensify Travel.
```

This newly-added justification comment (the second `connectWithoutView is justified` block) joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...preview computation shared by LHN and Search; the caches were moved verbatim...`). Per the plain-comment-style guidance, a semicolon should be replaced with two separate sentences.

Split it into two sentences, e.g.:

```ts
// connectWithoutView is justified: this is module-level, non-render preview computation shared by LHN and Search.
// The caches were moved verbatim from OptionsListUtils. Migration to the derived value is tracked in issue #66381.
```

This newly-added justification comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon (`...preview computation shared by LHN and Search; the caches were moved verbatim...`). Per the plain-comment-style guidance, a semicolon should be replaced with two separate sentences.

Split it into two sentences, e.g.:

```ts
// connectWithoutView is justified: this is module-level, non-render preview computation shared by LHN and Search.
// The caches were moved verbatim from OptionsListUtils. Migration to the derived value is tracked in issue #66381.
```

This added comment uses an em dash ("...which lives outside this modal — preserve it, and drop focus from anything else"). Comments should avoid em dashes and read as plain sentences.

Suggested fix:

```ts
// The focus trap deactivates first (child effects run before ours) and may have already returned focus to
// the launcher, which lives outside this modal. Preserve it, and drop focus from anything else.
```

This added comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon ("What actually held focus wins; the anchor is the fallback..."). Use two separate sentences instead of a semicolon.

Suggested fix:

```ts
// What actually held focus wins. The anchor is the fallback for triggers that blur themselves
// before opening, which would otherwise leave us with `body` and no launcher at all.
```

This comment uses an arrow (`→`) to describe the `PublicScreens` to `AuthScreens` transition instead of writing the relationship out in plain words. Comments should read like natural sentences without arrows standing in for words.

Rewrite the relationship in words, for example:

```ts
// Prefer getRootState() over getState(): during the swap from PublicScreens to AuthScreens,
// getState() can briefly return a partial tree with routes but no routeNames, so
// navContainsProtectedRoutes stays false until an unrelated navigation fires. That delay
// is what lets a deferred deep-link navigate clobber the first Sign in / thread tap (#96346).
```

The added comment joins two independent sentences with a semicolon (`...parsed by \`new Date()\` as UTC midnight; formatting that instant...`). Comments should read as plain, natural sentences rather than using a semicolon to splice two thoughts together.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// A bare `yyyy-MM-dd` string is parsed by `new Date()` as UTC midnight. Formatting that instant
// in a timezone behind UTC rolls it back to the previous day, so these must skip straight to the
// `CSV_DATE_FORMATS` loop below, which parses `yyyy-MM-dd` as local time instead.
const ISO_DATE_ONLY_REGEX = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/;
```

The comment uses an em dash. Rewrite it as a plain sentence.

```tsx
// Keep the current selections available so they stay visible and removable, but don't offer a category that
// already has a tax default, since saving over it would silently replace the existing rule.
```

The comment joins two independent statements with a semicolon. Split them into two sentences.

```tsx
// Keep the row carrying the error rather than dropping it, so the failure is visible and can
// be dismissed. An update restores the stored rate. An add has none to fall back to and
// keeps its ADD pending action so dismissing the error removes the row.
```

This comment uses an em dash (`—`), which the plain-comment-style guideline disallows. Comments should read as plain, natural sentences without stylistic tics like em/en dashes.

Replace the em dash with plain wording, e.g.:

```tsx
// Rendered when the icons array is unexpectedly short. Falls back to the default user avatar.
const EMPTY_USER_ICON: Icon = {source: '', type: CONST.ICON_TYPE_AVATAR};
```

This newly added comment joins two independent clauses with a semicolon. Per the plain-comment-style guideline, comments should read as separate natural sentences rather than semicolon-spliced clauses.

Split it into two sentences:

```ts
// Bun resolves this module without a platform suffix. Web and native bundlers select their configured variants.
export default CONST_RUNTIME_DEFAULTS;
```

This comment uses a semicolon to splice two independent sentences together. Comments should read as plain sentences, so split them into two.

```tsx
// ...guided-setup tasks need loaded policies to resolve their deep links (https://github.com/Expensify/App/issues/71742). When isLoadingApp is true we skip and let
// ReportFetchHandler's deferred OpenReport run instead. Keep this guard as-is.
```

Comments should be plain sentences without em dashes. This added comment uses em dashes in its own prose ("...turns an O(reports × transactions × tags) pass — which froze the JS thread for a minute on very large tag lists — into O(affected transactions)."). The same applies to the em dash later in this block ("...the same 'has any enabled tag' state before/after — otherwise...").

Rewrite without em dashes, for example:

```ts
// those transactions turns an O(reports × transactions × tags) pass into O(affected
